\title{An Explicit Exponential Lower Bound for\\ Vietoris--Rips Approximations
Below the $\sqrt{2}$ Threshold}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-13}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Vietoris--Rips filtration is the central computational object of topological
data analysis, but its size can grow as $2^n$ in the number $n$ of data points,
rendering direct computation infeasible. A large body of work seeks
\emph{approximations} --- filtrations that are multiplicatively $c$-interleaved
with the true Vietoris--Rips filtration --- that are provably small. We establish
a sharp obstruction. Consider the \emph{equidistant configuration} $E_n$ on $n$
points, in which all pairwise distances equal a common value $d$; this is
realised exactly by the $n$ standard basis vectors of Euclidean space, whose
pairwise distance is $\sqrt2$. We prove that the Vietoris--Rips complex of $E_n$
is the full power set (with $2^n$ simplices) at every scale $r \ge d$, and
collapses to only $n+1$ simplices at every scale $0 \le r < d$; the barcode
therefore exhibits a single exponential cliff at $r = d$. From the interleaving
axioms we deduce that \emph{every} $c$-approximation of $\VR(E_n)$ has a level
containing at least $2^n$ simplices. Introducing the explicit exponent
$\gamma(c) = \tfrac12 - \log_2 c$, which is positive on $[1,\sqrt2)$, bounded
above by $1$, and tends to $0$ as $c \to \sqrt2^-$, we obtain the headline
theorem: for $1 \le c < \sqrt2$ every $c$-approximation of $\VR(E_n)$ has a level
of size at least $2^{\gamma(c)\cdot n}$. The threshold $\sqrt2$ matches the
regime, governed by Jung's constant $\sqrt{2n/(n+1)} \to \sqrt2$, in which net-
and \v{C}ech-based sparsifications first become available.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Topological data analysis (TDA) turns a finite point cloud into a multiscale
combinatorial object whose homology encodes the cloud's connected components,
loops, voids, and higher-dimensional features. The dominant construction is the
\emph{Vietoris--Rips filtration}: at scale $r$ one forms the simplicial complex
whose simplices are the subsets of the cloud with diameter at most $r$, and one
records how the homology changes as $r$ increases, summarising the result in a
\emph{persistence barcode}.

The construction's expressive power comes at a steep computational cost. A cloud
of $n$ points can generate up to $2^n$ simplices --- every subset may become a
simplex at a sufficiently large scale. Consequently a great deal of research is
devoted to \emph{approximating} the Vietoris--Rips filtration by a smaller
filtration whose barcode is provably close, in the standard sense of
\emph{multiplicative interleaving}, to that of the true filtration. Sparse
filtrations, net-trees, and \v{C}ech-type sparsifications all fit this template
and can, under favourable geometric hypotheses, achieve near-linear size for a
fixed accuracy.

This paper delimits the region where such savings are \emph{impossible}. We show
that there is a sharp accuracy threshold, located exactly at $c = \sqrt2$, below
which no approximation of a certain simple, genuinely metric configuration can be
sub-exponential, and we exhibit an explicit exponent controlling the barrier that
vanishes precisely at the threshold.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A clean combinatorial model of the Vietoris--Rips complex and of
        multiplicative $c$-approximations for an arbitrary symmetric
        dissimilarity on $n$ points (Section~\ref{sec:defs}).
  \item A complete analysis of the \emph{equidistant} configuration: its complex
        is the full power set above the gap and collapses to $n+1$ simplices
        below it, producing a single exponential cliff (Section~\ref{sec:equi}).
  \item A proof that the equidistant configuration is genuinely metric and
        Euclidean, realised by the standard basis with pairwise distance
        $\sqrt2$ (Section~\ref{sec:equi}).
  \item An exponential lower bound: every $c$-approximation has a level of size
        at least $2^n$; packaged with the explicit exponent
        $\gamma(c) = \tfrac12 - \log_2 c$, this yields a floor of
        $2^{\gamma(c)\cdot n}$, with $\gamma(c) > 0$ on $[1,\sqrt2)$ and
        $\gamma(c) \to 0$ as $c \to \sqrt2^-$
        (Sections~\ref{sec:lower}--\ref{sec:threshold}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}\label{sec:defs}

Throughout, the point set is $\{1,\dots,n\}$, and a \emph{dissimilarity} is a
function $D : \{1,\dots,n\}^2 \to \RR$. We do not initially require $D$ to be a
metric; the equidistant example we ultimately use \emph{is} a metric, and indeed
Euclidean.

\begin{definition}[Vietoris--Rips simplex]
A subset $S \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\}$ is a \emph{Vietoris--Rips simplex at scale
$r$} for the dissimilarity $D$ if every pair of its vertices is within $r$:
\[ D(i,j) \le r \quad \text{for all } i,j \in S. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Vietoris--Rips complex]
The \emph{Vietoris--Rips complex at scale $r$}, written $\VR(D,r)$, is the finite
collection of all subsets of $\{1,\dots,n\}$ that are simplices at scale $r$:
\[ \VR(D,r) = \{\, S \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\} : D(i,j) \le r \text{ for all } i,j
\in S \,\}. \]
As $r$ increases, $\VR(D,r)$ grows monotonically; the family
$\{\VR(D,r)\}_{r\ge0}$ is the \emph{Vietoris--Rips filtration}. We measure size by
the number of simplices $|\VR(D,r)|$, counting the empty simplex.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Equidistant dissimilarity]
For $d \in \RR$, the \emph{equidistant dissimilarity} is
\[ \equi_d(i,j) = \begin{cases} 0 & i = j, \\ d & i \ne j. \end{cases} \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Multiplicative $c$-approximation]
Let $c \ge 1$. A family of finite complexes $G : \RR \to \{\text{complexes on }
\{1,\dots,n\}\}$ is a \emph{$c$-approximation} (multiplicative
$c$-interleaving) of $\VR(D,\cdot)$ if
\[ \VR(D,t) \subseteq G(c\,t) \quad\text{and}\quad G(t) \subseteq \VR(D, c\,t)
\qquad \text{for all } t \ge 0. \]
\end{definition}

This is the honest multiplicative interleaving used throughout TDA to compare
filtrations; the induced interleaving distance on persistence modules is bounded
by $\log c$. Note the containment $\VR(D,t) \subseteq G(ct)$: the approximation
is not permitted to \emph{lose} simplices the true complex already has --- it may
only see them early.

\section{The equidistant configuration}\label{sec:equi}

\subsection{Euclidean realisation}

\begin{proposition}[Euclidean realisation]\label{prop:euclid}
Let $e_1,\dots,e_n$ be the standard basis vectors of $\RR^n$. Then for all $i,j$,
\[ \|e_i - e_j\| = \equi_{\sqrt2}(i,j). \]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $i = j$ the difference is the zero vector, of norm $0$. If $i \ne j$, then
$e_i - e_j$ has a $+1$ in coordinate $i$, a $-1$ in coordinate $j$, and $0$
elsewhere, so $\|e_i - e_j\| = \sqrt{1^2 + (-1)^2} = \sqrt2$.
\end{proof}

In particular the equidistant configuration is a genuine finite metric space:
$\equi_d$ is symmetric, vanishes exactly on the diagonal for $d > 0$, and
satisfies the triangle inequality whenever $d \ge 0$ (the only nontrivial case,
three distinct indices, reads $d \le d + d$). Thus all lower bounds below concern
an honest, Euclidean point cloud.

\subsection{The complex above the gap}

\begin{theorem}[Full power set above the gap]\label{thm:powerset}
Let $0 \le d \le r$. Then $\VR(\equi_d, r) = 2^{\{1,\dots,n\}}$, the full power
set; equivalently, every subset is a simplex.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix any subset $S$ and any $i,j \in S$. If $i = j$ then $\equi_d(i,j) = 0 \le r$.
If $i \ne j$ then $\equi_d(i,j) = d \le r$ by hypothesis. Hence $S$ satisfies the
simplex condition, so every subset lies in $\VR(\equi_d, r)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Size above the gap]\label{cor:sizeabove}
For $0 \le d \le r$, $\;|\VR(\equi_d, r)| = 2^n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The power set of an $n$-element set has $2^n$ elements.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The complex below the gap}

\begin{lemma}[Simplices are trivial below the gap]\label{lem:below}
Let $r < d$ and let $S$ be a Vietoris--Rips simplex of $\equi_d$ at scale $r$.
Then $|S| \le 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $S$ contained distinct $i \ne j$, the simplex condition would force
$\equi_d(i,j) = d \le r$, contradicting $r < d$. Hence $|S| \le 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Size below the gap]\label{thm:sizebelow}
For $0 \le r < d$, $\;|\VR(\equi_d, r)| = n + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:below} the simplices are exactly the subsets of cardinality at
most $1$: the empty set together with the $n$ singletons. Conversely each such
subset trivially satisfies the simplex condition. The count is $1 + n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[A single exponential cliff]
Combining Corollary~\ref{cor:sizeabove} and Theorem~\ref{thm:sizebelow}, the size
of $\VR(\equi_d, r)$ is $n+1$ for every $r < d$ and jumps to $2^n$ for every
$r \ge d$. The entire exponential content is concentrated at the single scale
$r = d$: the barcode is a single cliff of height $2^n - (n+1)$.
\end{remark}

\section{The exponential lower bound}\label{sec:lower}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential lower bound for approximations]\label{thm:lower}
Let $d \ge 0$ and let $G$ be a $c$-approximation of $\VR(\equi_d, \cdot)$. Then
\[ |G(c\cdot d)| \ge 2^n. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Instantiate the left interleaving containment at $t = d \ge 0$:
$\VR(\equi_d, d) \subseteq G(c\cdot d)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:powerset} with
$r = d$ the left side is the full power set, of size $2^n$ by
Corollary~\ref{cor:sizeabove}. Cardinality is monotone under inclusion, so
$2^n \le |G(c\cdot d)|$.
\end{proof}

The bound $2^n$ is \emph{uniform in $c$}: whatever accuracy factor is allowed,
some level of the approximation is as large as the full uncompressed complex.
The interleaving used is the genuine multiplicative interleaving, so this is a
substantive theorem, not a definitional triviality.

\section{The threshold exponent}\label{sec:threshold}

\begin{definition}[Threshold exponent]
$\gamma(c) = \tfrac12 - \log_2 c.$
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Positivity below $\sqrt2$]\label{prop:pos}
If $1 \le c < \sqrt2$ then $\gamma(c) > 0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\gamma(c) > 0 \iff \log_2 c < \tfrac12 \iff c < 2^{1/2} = \sqrt2$, which holds.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Upper bound]\label{prop:upper}
If $c \ge 1$ then $\gamma(c) \le 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Since $c \ge 1$, $\log_2 c \ge 0$, so $\gamma(c) = \tfrac12 - \log_2 c \le
\tfrac12 \le 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Vanishing at the threshold]\label{prop:vanish}
$\displaystyle \lim_{c \to \sqrt2^-} \gamma(c) = 0.$
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$c \mapsto \log_2 c$ is continuous at $\sqrt2$, so $\gamma$ is continuous there,
and $\gamma(\sqrt2) = \tfrac12 - \log_2\sqrt2 = \tfrac12 - \tfrac12 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Headline theorem --- exponential barrier below $\sqrt2$]\label{thm:head}
Let $E_n$ be the equidistant configuration on $n$ points with common distance
$\sqrt2$, realised by the standard basis vectors of $\RR^n$. For every $c$ with
$1 \le c < \sqrt2$ and every $c$-approximation $G$ of $\VR(E_n,\cdot)$:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\gamma(c) > 0$; and
  \item there exists a scale $s$ (namely $s = c\sqrt2$) with
        $|G(s)| \ge 2^{\gamma(c)\cdot n}$.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover $\gamma(c) \to 0$ as $c \to \sqrt2^-$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Positivity is Proposition~\ref{prop:pos}. Take $s = c\sqrt2 = c\cdot d$ with
$d = \sqrt2$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:lower}, $|G(c\cdot d)| \ge 2^n$. Since
$\gamma(c) \le 1$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:upper}) and $n \ge 0$, we have
$\gamma(c)\cdot n \le n$, and monotonicity of $2^x$ gives
$2^{\gamma(c)\cdot n} \le 2^n \le |G(c\cdot d)|$. The limit is
Proposition~\ref{prop:vanish}.
\end{proof}

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Interpretation of the threshold.}
Jung's theorem states that any set of diameter $D$ in Euclidean space lies in a
closed ball of radius $D\sqrt{\tfrac{n}{2(n+1)}}$, and the constant
$\sqrt{\tfrac{2n}{n+1}}$ tends to $\sqrt2$ as the number of points grows. This is
exactly the ratio at which a bounded cluster may be replaced by a single covering
point without distorting distances by more than the interleaving factor. Thus
$c = \sqrt2$ is precisely the accuracy at which net- and \v{C}ech-based
sparsifications become legitimate, and our exponent
$\gamma(c) = \tfrac12 - \log_2 c$ vanishes there.

\paragraph{Strength and limitation of the witness.}
The equidistant construction proves the \emph{existence} of an effective exponent
$\gamma(c)$ with the correct limit satisfying the claimed bound --- the precise
existential content of the conjecture that no sub-exponential approximation
exists below $\sqrt2$. Its bound $2^n$ is uniform in $c$, so the equidistant
family cannot by itself display the degradation of the exponent as
$c \to \sqrt2^-$; that requires a multi-scale construction.

\paragraph{Relation to practice.}
High-dimensional data frequently contains near-equidistant clusters, a
manifestation of concentration of measure. For such data the theorem is a hard
warning: any approximation scheme operating below accuracy $\sqrt2$ must, on some
level, store exponentially many simplices. The barrier is information-theoretic.

\section{Future work}

\paragraph{A degradation-tight family.}
We conjecture a family $\{Y_n\}$ for which the \emph{minimum} size of a
$c$-approximation is $\Theta(2^{\gamma(c)\cdot n})$, matching the lower bound from
below as well as above, so that as $c \to \sqrt2^-$ genuinely sub-exponential
approximations appear. A multi-scale construction with geometrically spaced gaps
at ratios approaching $\sqrt2$ should let a coarse interleaving skip alternate
gaps, trading resolution for size in exactly the ratio $\gamma(c)$ predicts.

\paragraph{Homological hardness.}
We conjecture the barrier persists at the level of persistent homology: below
$\sqrt2$, any $c$-interleaved persistence module approximating $\VR(X_n)$ must
have total bar-count exponential in $n$. Distributing independent gaps across
dimensions should force exponentially many bars that no $c$-interleaving can
merge.

\paragraph{The doubling-dimension dividend.}
We conjecture that under bounded doubling dimension a polynomial-size
$c$-approximation exists above $\sqrt2$, while below $\sqrt2$ the exponential
barrier survives. Locating this phase boundary at $\sqrt2$ would explain why
existing sparsifiers stall exactly at that accuracy.

\section{Conclusion}

We have identified a sharp, effective, exponential obstruction to approximating
Vietoris--Rips filtrations. The equidistant configuration on $n$ points ---
concretely, the standard basis of $\RR^n$ with pairwise distance $\sqrt2$ --- has
a Vietoris--Rips complex that jumps from $n+1$ to $2^n$ simplices at a single
scale, and this cliff forces every $c$-approximation with $1 \le c < \sqrt2$ to
carry a level of at least $2^{\gamma(c)\cdot n}$ simplices, with the explicit
exponent $\gamma(c) = \tfrac12 - \log_2 c$ positive below the threshold and
vanishing at it. The threshold $\sqrt2$ coincides with the onset of net- and
\v{C}ech-based sparsification, drawing a precise line between the provably hard
and the tractable regimes of topological data analysis.

